// WHY_L16 — reasoning ("why") questions for Day 16 (10/19), treating poisonings II (Dr. Smith), and for her
// poisoning treatment table key (TBL3). Keyed on her 10/19 words, then the deck slide, then her key
// (PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf). No TOPICS entry: L16-1xx use the L16 subs, TBL3-1xx the TBL3 subs.
QUESTIONS.push(
/* ================= Part (a): L16 lecture reasons ================= */
{id:"L16-101", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"absorbrev", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-mdac-enterohepatic", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is multiple-dose activated charcoal tried for a poison that undergoes enterohepatic recirculation?",
 options:[
  {t:"The poison returns to the small intestine, where a later dose can catch it", correct:true, why:"Her answer, after Anna's: the next dose, and maybe a third, is given while the poison is being re-injected into the small intestine with the bile, so the charcoal grabs a little more and it is fecally eliminated."},
  {t:"The first dose saturates, so each later dose binds the same poison more tightly", correct:false, why:"Treats the problem as charcoal capacity; the doses are about timing, meeting poison that comes back into the small intestine with the bile after the first dose has moved on."},
  {t:"Repeated doses bind the poison in the blood and carry it to the kidney", correct:false, why:"Charcoal works in the gut, and the poison it grabs is fecally eliminated, not renally."},
  {t:"Repeated doses make the urine basic so the poison is trapped there", correct:false, why:"That is ion trapping with intravenous sodium bicarbonate, a different kinetic treatment."},
  {t:"Repeated doses speed gastric emptying so the poison passes out sooner", correct:false, why:"Pushing contents through the gut is whole bowel irrigation, not charcoal."}],
 teach:"Some compounds are absorbed from the gut, go to the liver, are put into the bile and are re-injected into the small intestine (enterohepatic recirculation). If such a compound is grabbed by charcoal, giving a second and third dose while it is back in the small intestine lets the charcoal capture more of it. The charcoal-bound poison then leaves in the feces. The slide adds that multiple-dose charcoal can be used, when appropriate, once a toxin has been absorbed.",
 quote:"If they're a compound that will do that recirculation and is grabbed by charcoal, you can go ahead and try the multiple dose activated charcoal because you try to give the next dose, and maybe a third dose while the medication is, or the poison is being re-injected back into the small intestine. Hopefully, you can grab a little more, right?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’ (multiple-dose activated charcoal); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-102", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"absorbrev", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-ed-tries-risky-methods", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Charcoal has the best outcome data, yet an emergency department (ED) team may still try emesis, gastric lavage or whole bowel irrigation. Which explains this?",
 options:[
  {t:"The overdose is so severe the patient may not otherwise be saved", correct:true, why:"Her answer to her own question (\"Will they try that? Yeah, why not?\"): when someone has really overdosed and probably cannot be saved, the other absorption-prevention options are tried despite their risks."},
  {t:"These methods carry no risk when done in a hospital", correct:false, why:"All of the methods carry some risk; emesis has aspiration as its biggest risk."},
  {t:"Their outcome data are better than the data for charcoal", correct:false, why:"The outcome data favor charcoal."},
  {t:"They must be done first to empty the stomach for charcoal", correct:false, why:"Treats the methods as a sequence; she presented each as a separate way to prevent absorption, with charcoal best supported by outcome data and useful up to about 2 hours after ingestion."},
  {t:"Charcoal can no longer be used once the patient reaches the hospital", correct:false, why:"Charcoal is the hospital step; the guidelines say it is done at the hospital, not at home."}],
 teach:"Every absorption-prevention method carries risk (aspiration is the biggest risk of emesis), and case outcome data favor charcoal. But charcoal does not grab everything a person can ingest, and it only works while the poison is still in the stomach and small intestine. In a severe overdose where the patient probably cannot otherwise be saved, the chance that a riskier method removes more poison is worth its risk, so the team tries it; the decision rests on severity, not on better evidence.",
 quote:"Will they try that? Yeah, why not? If, if somebody has really overdosed on something, and you probably can't save them, then, you know, the risks might outweigh the benefit for using some of these other options to prevent absorption.",
 note:"Her words say \"the risks might outweigh the benefit\"; her answer to the question (\"Yeah, why not?\") is that the team will try them in a severe case. Keyed on the answer she gave: in a severe case the riskier methods are worth trying.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’ (review); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-103", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-guidelines-from-case-data", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why are the out-of-hospital guidance documents for beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose built from published cases and case series rather than clinical trials?",
 options:[
  {t:"No one can be enrolled in a trial to be poisoned", correct:true, why:"Her reason: we cannot run clinical trials and sign people up to volunteer to get poisoned, so the outcomes data come from people being treated and then written up."},
  {t:"Case reports rank above trials in evidence-based methods", correct:false, why:"The guidelines rank the data they have; case data are used because trials cannot be done, not because they rank higher."},
  {t:"Trials exist, but case data are cheaper to collect", correct:false, why:"Assumes trials are possible; they are not, since no one can be signed up to be poisoned, so case data are the only data."},
  {t:"Poison centers write the documents from their own phone calls only", correct:false, why:"The authors pulled published cases and case reports from the literature."},
  {t:"The 2005 documents predate clinical trials", correct:false, why:"Confuses age with method; the documents are still current, and their data come from cases because poisoning trials cannot be run at any date."}],
 teach:"People cannot be recruited to be poisoned, so there are no clinical trials of poisoning treatments. The data come from patients who were treated and whose cases or case series were published. Guideline authors pull all of those cases, evaluate them with evidence-based methods, rank the data and the strength of each recommendation, and write the guidance documents.",
 quote:"we really can't run clinical trials and sign up people to volunteer to get poisoned and try to see what treatment works best for them so the outcomes data we have is really from people being treated and then we study, gather the data on how that treatment worked and people post cases or case series they publish those kinds of papers",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Guidelines for Overdoses’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-104", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-methanol-in-alcohol-use-disorder", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Most methanol exposures are in people with an alcohol use disorder. Which explains why they drink it, and why it ends badly?",
 options:[
  {t:"It gives an ethanol-like effect but is metabolized to a blinding compound", correct:true, why:"Her account: when ethanol is not available they sometimes use methanol and get the same kind of effect, except that it is metabolized to a nasty compound that can cause blindness."},
  {t:"It tastes sweet and is mistaken for a soft drink, then crystallizes in the kidneys", correct:false, why:"Sweet taste and crystals in small vessels belong to ethylene glycol (antifreeze)."},
  {t:"It is used to ease alcohol withdrawal, and then it blocks alcohol dehydrogenase", correct:false, why:"Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase is what ethanol and fomepizole do as antidotes."},
  {t:"It is safe until it is mixed with ethanol in the stomach", correct:false, why:"Ethanol is an antidote for methanol, not what makes it toxic."},
  {t:"It is cleared unchanged by the kidneys and causes acidosis there", correct:false, why:"Methanol is harmful through its metabolism to formic acid."}],
 teach:"Ethylene glycol exposures are mostly accidental; methanol exposures are mostly in people with an alcohol use disorder who cannot get ethanol. Methanol gives them an alcohol effect, but alcohol dehydrogenase and formaldehyde dehydrogenase turn it into formic acid. Formic acid damages the retina, and sight is lost.",
 quote:"the methanol exposure, most of the time, those are people that have an alcohol substance use disorder. They're alcoholics, and maybe they can't get ethanol, and they'll sometimes use methanol, OK? And so they're, they'll be exposed to an alcohol, they get the same kind of, uh, Uh, effect that ethanol would give them, except for they're metabolizing it to a nasty compound that can cause blindness.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-105", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"methanol-eg-antidote-adh", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient who drank methanol is given fomepizole before much of it has been metabolized. What would happen to formic acid formation, and why does that matter?",
 options:[
  {t:"Little formic acid forms, so the retina is spared", correct:true, why:"Fomepizole binds and inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, which blocks the bioactivation of methanol; formic acid is the compound that kills the receptors at the back of the retina."},
  {t:"Formic acid forms faster and is cleared sooner", correct:false, why:"Speeding metabolism is the cofactor approach; here the goal is to stop a toxic metabolite from forming."},
  {t:"Formic acid still forms, but fomepizole binds it in the blood", correct:false, why:"Binding a toxin in the blood is inactivation; fomepizole acts on the enzyme."},
  {t:"Formic acid still forms, but its renal elimination is ion-trapped", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is a different kinetic treatment."},
  {t:"Formic acid formation is unchanged; only formaldehyde is blocked", correct:false, why:"Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase stops the first step, so neither formaldehyde nor formic acid is made from it."}],
 teach:"Methanol goes to formaldehyde by alcohol dehydrogenase and then to formic acid by formaldehyde dehydrogenase. Formic acid is the toxic product, and it damages the retina. Ethanol (by outcompeting) or fomepizole (by binding and inhibiting) blocks alcohol dehydrogenase, so the toxic metabolites are not made; this is the kinetic approach of inhibiting bioactivation.",
 quote:"Same thing with fomeprazole. It'll, it'll bind to and inhibit the alcohol dehydrogenase and prevent the metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol. So that's the way we block. The metabolism and bioactivation of these two compounds to nasty metabolites.",
 note:"The transcript renders fomepizole as \"omeprazole\" and \"fomeprazole\"; the slide has fomepizole.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-106", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"detect", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-poison-identity-a-guess", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why is identifying what a poisoned patient took sometimes only a guess?",
 options:[
  {t:"Patients may not say, and labs may not test for it", correct:true, why:"Her reasons: people who intend to hurt themselves often do not say what they ingested, others are unconscious, and a blood test confirms the suspicion only if the hospital lab can test for that compound."},
  {t:"Toxidromes are identical for every class of poison", correct:false, why:"A toxidrome is a group of symptoms that points toward a particular compound."},
  {t:"Blood levels cannot be measured for any common overdose", correct:false, why:"Emergency rooms commonly check the blood for acetaminophen, salicylates, iron, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and opiates."},
  {t:"A blood draw always names the poison within minutes of arrival", correct:false, why:"Overstates the test: a blood draw only confirms a compound that was suspected and that the hospital lab is able to test for."},
  {t:"Symptoms never appear until after the antidote is given", correct:false, why:"The first guess is made from the symptoms the patient displays."}],
 teach:"The first guess comes from the symptoms, often a toxidrome, the group of symptoms that suggests a particular compound. A blood draw then confirms it if the hospital laboratory can test for the compound suspected. When the patient will not say what they took, is unconscious, or the lab cannot test for it, the identity stays a guess, which is what makes poisonings hard to treat.",
 quote:"Which, which, which is what makes treating toxicology, uh, you know, poisoning so hard is people that intend to hurt themselves, they don't, they don't tell you often what they've ingested, right? Other times people are unconscious and they can't tell you, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxidromes’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-107", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-enhance-metabolism-condition", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Speeding up the metabolism of a poison is a useful treatment only under one condition. Which one?",
 options:[
  {t:"The metabolism must not make another toxic metabolite", correct:true, why:"Her condition: the poison is metabolized faster and cleared, if that metabolism does not create another toxic metabolite."},
  {t:"The poison must be weakly acidic", correct:false, why:"Weak acids are the condition for ion trapping in the urine."},
  {t:"The poison must still be in the stomach", correct:false, why:"That is the condition for charcoal."},
  {t:"The enzyme must be inducible within a few hours", correct:false, why:"Induction takes days, so it is not used; cofactors are given instead."},
  {t:"The poison must have a small volume of distribution in the body", correct:false, why:"That is the condition for the extracorporeal methods."}],
 teach:"Enhancing metabolism (detoxification) aims to get the body to clear a poison faster. It only helps if the metabolism does not make another toxic metabolite; methanol and ethylene glycol are the opposite case, where metabolism is blocked instead. Since enzymes cannot be induced fast enough, the speed-up comes from giving cofactors, as with cyanide and acetaminophen.",
 quote:"Maybe you can get it to be metabolized faster and cleared from the body. If that metabolism doesn't create another toxic metabolite, right?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Metabolism’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-108", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-cyanide-not-finished-at-methemoglobin", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"In cyanide poisoning, methemoglobin has pulled the cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase and cellular respiration resumes. Why is the treatment not finished?",
 options:[
  {t:"The cyanide is still in the body, now on methemoglobin", correct:true, why:"Her answer: we still have cyanide in their body; bound to methemoglobin it is not as damaging as on cytochrome c oxidase, but it has to be gotten out of the system entirely."},
  {t:"Cytochrome c oxidase stays permanently inactivated", correct:false, why:"Pulling the cyanide off regenerates cytochrome c oxidase; permanent binding is the aging story for organophosphates."},
  {t:"Amyl nitrite is now the main toxin left in the blood", correct:false, why:"The remaining problems are the cyanide on methemoglobin and the methemoglobin itself."},
  {t:"Methemoglobin breaks down and releases free cyanide into the urine", correct:false, why:"The cyanide leaves in the urine only after rhodanese helps move it onto thiosulfate."},
  {t:"Thiosulfate has already carried the cyanide to the kidney", correct:false, why:"Skips a step: the cyanide reaches the kidney only after rhodanese, using sulfur from thiosulfate, converts it to thiocyanate."}],
 teach:"Amyl nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, and methemoglobin pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase, so the cell can make ATP again. The cyanide is now on methemoglobin, which is less damaging but still in the body. Rhodanese, the body's own enzyme, then moves it onto sulfur from thiosulfate (given as sodium thiosulfate), and the product is renally eliminated.",
 quote:"Have we finished the process of saving them? Not really. We still have cyanide in their body. It is bound to methemoglobin, which isn't quite so damaging as being bound to cytochrome C oxidase, but you really want to get the cyanide out of their system entirely.",
 note:"The cyanide figure is from Golan's textbook and did not survive the deck text export.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-109", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"rhodanese-enhance-metabolism-step", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the cyanide scheme, why does giving sodium thiosulfate speed the clearance of cyanide?",
 options:[
  {t:"It supplies sulfur for the body's rhodanese", correct:true, why:"Rhodanese, an enzyme the body already has, converts cyanide to thiocyanate, which is renally eliminated; the pathway is capacity limited, and thiosulfate donates the sulfur group so more thiocyanate is made."},
  {t:"It pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase", correct:false, why:"Methemoglobin, made by amyl nitrite, does that; thiosulfate acts later, when the cyanide is cleared."},
  {t:"It is the enzyme that makes thiocyanate", correct:false, why:"Rhodanese is the enzyme; thiosulfate is the sulfur donor it uses."},
  {t:"It converts methemoglobin back to hemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue, a reducing agent, does that."},
  {t:"It oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Amyl nitrite does that."}],
 teach:"Rhodanese is an enzyme the body already has: it converts cyanide to thiocyanate, a much less toxic product that is renally eliminated. The pathway is capacity limited, so on its own it clears cyanide slowly. Giving sodium thiosulfate donates the sulfur group rhodanese needs, so more thiocyanate is made. Speeding a detoxifying reaction by supplying what it needs, rather than inducing the enzyme, is the enhance-metabolism step.",
 quote:"So by giving rodonase as a cofactor, we pull the cyanide off of methemoglobin onto the thiosulfate, and this will be renally eliminated.",
 note:"She said \"giving rodonase as a cofactor\" and \"thiosulfate is freely in the body\". Read in context, rhodanese is the enzyme the step needs and is already in the body; the agent given is sodium thiosulfate, as in the assigned Marraffa article: \"Rhodanese, an endogenous enzyme, then facilitates the formation of thiocyanate\" (p. 203) and \"Thiosulfate enhances the activity of rhodanese by donating a sulfur group\" (p. 204).",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slides ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Metabolism’, ‘Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19; Marraffa+AJHSP+2012+Antidotes.pdf pp. 203-204"},
{id:"L16-110", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-methylene-blue-after-cyanide", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"The cyanide has been moved onto thiosulfate and is being renally eliminated. What problem can you still see in this patient, and why must it be treated?",
 options:[
  {t:"High methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen", correct:true, why:"Her answer: they still have high levels of methemoglobin; blood cannot carry oxygen when the hemoglobin is in that form, so methylene blue is given to convert it back."},
  {t:"Thiosulfate-cyanide building up in the blood", correct:false, why:"The thiosulfate-cyanide product is renally eliminated."},
  {t:"Cytochrome c oxidase still blocked by cyanide", correct:false, why:"Methemoglobin already pulled the cyanide off the enzyme."},
  {t:"Too much rhodanese oxidizing the hemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Amyl nitrite made the methemoglobin; rhodanese is the body's enzyme that moves cyanide onto sulfur from thiosulfate."},
  {t:"Free cyanide released from the thiosulfate", correct:false, why:"Assumes the thiosulfate-cyanide product falls apart; it leaves the body in the urine."}],
 teach:"The treatment deliberately made methemoglobin, which does not carry oxygen. Once the cyanide is cleared, the patient still has high methemoglobin. Methylene blue, a reducing agent, converts methemoglobin back to hemoglobin so the blood can carry oxygen again.",
 quote:"What problem can you still see in this patient? Uh, right here. Bless you, bless you. They still have high levels of methemoglobin, right? And that's not good either. Your blood can't carry oxygen if the hemoglobin in red blood cells is in the form of methemoglobin.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-111", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"nac-glutathione-ingredients", dupOf:"L16-019", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"The body makes glutathione all the time. Why does giving N-acetylcysteine (NAC) still speed the clearance of NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen?",
 options:[
  {t:"It supplies ingredients to make more glutathione", correct:true, why:"Her answer: NAC provides some of the ingredients needed to create more glutathione, which conjugates NAPQI with the GST enzymes; NAC acts as a cofactor."},
  {t:"NAC neutralizes the NAPQI free radical directly", correct:false, why:"A student's answer she rejected: \"No, it's not neutralizing free radicals. Good, good point though\"."},
  {t:"NAC blocks the enzyme that turns acetaminophen into NAPQI", correct:false, why:"Blocking bioactivation is the methanol and ethylene glycol approach, not the acetaminophen one."},
  {t:"NAC induces the glutathione S-transferases within an hour", correct:false, why:"Enzyme induction takes days, so it is not used in a poisoning."},
  {t:"NAC makes the urine basic so NAPQI is trapped there", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is used for weak acids such as salicylates."}],
 teach:"Acetaminophen is metabolized to NAPQI, the free radical that damages the liver. Glutathione, with the GST enzymes, conjugates NAPQI. The body makes glutathione constantly, but giving NAC provides ingredients to make more of it, so NAC works like a cofactor that enhances the metabolism and eventual elimination of NAPQI.",
 quote:"No, it's not neutralizing free radicals. Good, good point though ... But one of the things we can do in this situation is give NA andactylcysteine, cause NA provides some of the ingredients needed to create more glutathione.",
 note:"The transcript renders NAPQI as \"NAQI\" and N-acetylcysteine as \"anastylcysteine\" and \"NA andactylcysteine\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Acetaminophen Example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-112", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"renal", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-no-cranberry-for-bases", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Cranberry juice is known to acidify urine. Why is it not used to ion-trap a weakly basic poison?",
 options:[
  {t:"It does not lower urine pH enough or fast enough", correct:true, why:"Her reason: you do not get much acidification of the urine from cranberry juice, not a low enough pH and not fast enough for someone who has ingested a basic poison."},
  {t:"It makes the urine more basic, not more acidic", correct:false, why:"She described it as known to acidify urine, only not enough."},
  {t:"It acidifies the blood to a dangerous degree", correct:false, why:"Acidifying the blood is the danger of a stronger approach; the juice fails for being too weak."},
  {t:"It ion-traps acidic poisons, not basic ones", correct:false, why:"Reverses the rule: acidic urine would charge a basic poison; acidic poisons are trapped in basic urine made with sodium bicarbonate."},
  {t:"A basic poison carries no charge at any urine pH, acidic or basic", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping depends on pH changing a compound's charge; the obstacle for bases is reaching a low enough urine pH safely and quickly."}],
 teach:"Ion trapping works well for weakly acidic poisons: intravenous sodium bicarbonate makes the urine basic, the acid becomes charged and is not reabsorbed. For weakly basic poisons the urine would have to be made acidic, and acidifying a person's blood to do that is dangerous. Cranberry juice acidifies urine only a little and too slowly to treat a poisoning.",
 quote:"you really don't get a lot of acidification of the urine by drinking cranberry juice, not high enough or not, not low enough pH and not fast enough for treating somebody that has ingested a basic type of poison",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Renal Elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-113", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-inactivator-high-affinity", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why must an inactivator have high affinity for the toxin it binds?",
 options:[
  {t:"Weak binding would need massive doses with side effects", correct:true, why:"Her reason: you do not want to give massive amounts of an inactivator, which on its own could cause side effects."},
  {t:"High affinity lets the complex be cleared by ion trapping", correct:false, why:"Clearance of the complex is a separate requirement, and ion trapping is not involved."},
  {t:"High affinity lets the inactivator reach the toxin's target first", correct:false, why:"The inactivator binds the toxin, not its target."},
  {t:"High affinity keeps the toxin from being absorbed from the gut", correct:false, why:"Inactivation applies after the poison has been absorbed into the circulation."},
  {t:"High affinity turns the toxin into a less toxic metabolite", correct:false, why:"Making a metabolite is metabolism; an inactivator binds the toxin."}],
 teach:"An inactivator binds the toxin and prevents it from interacting with its target, and the complex is then cleared. If it bound the toxin weakly, large amounts would be needed, and large amounts of the inactivator could cause side effects of their own. So the slide lists high affinity for the toxin as a requirement.",
 quote:"a good inactivator needs to have really good affinity for the toxin. You don't want to have to give massive amounts of an inactivator, which maybe on its own could cause side effects.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-114", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-inactivator-must-cover-poison", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"An attempted inactivator binds only a small part of a poison molecule. What would happen?",
 options:[
  {t:"The free part of the poison may still reach its target", correct:true, why:"Her example: if the inactivator binds only a little bit, this part of the poison may be active still and find the target."},
  {t:"The poison would be fully inactivated but cleared slowly", correct:false, why:"Assumes any binding blocks activity; the part of the poison left uncovered can still find its target."},
  {t:"The poison would be bioactivated to a toxic metabolite", correct:false, why:"Mixes in bioactivation (methanol, ethylene glycol); an inactivator acts by binding, and the problem here is the uncovered part of the poison."},
  {t:"The complex would bind calcium instead of the poison", correct:false, why:"Binding calcium is the chelator selectivity concern, a separate point."},
  {t:"Nothing; any binding at all inactivates the poison", correct:false, why:"She said the inactivator has to grab the poison so it cannot touch the target."}],
 teach:"An inactivator works by binding the toxin so the toxin cannot interact with its target. If it covers only part of the molecule, the free part may still act on the target. That is why she changed the slide's last bullet from \"low\" to \"no\" toxicity for the toxin-inactivator complex.",
 quote:"Uh, an attempted inactivator, let's say that only binds a little bit, this part of the poison may be active still, and it's finding the target. So that inactivator has to grab it in such a way that poison can't be touching the target",
 note:"The slide says the complex must have low toxicity; she said to change that to no toxicity. Keyed on her words.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-115", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-chelator-low-calcium-affinity", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why should an ideal heavy metal chelator have low binding affinity for calcium?",
 options:[
  {t:"It would bind up ions the body needs", correct:true, why:"Her reason: you do not want it to bind up the things you need; it should bind the metal the patient was poisoned with. Calcium is the most common concern."},
  {t:"Calcium would make the chelator toxic", correct:false, why:"The concern is losing the body's calcium, not the chelator becoming toxic."},
  {t:"Calcium would compete the metal off the chelator", correct:false, why:"Turns the concern around: the worry is the chelator taking the body's calcium, not calcium displacing the toxic metal."},
  {t:"Calcium is the metal most often chelated", correct:false, why:"The chelators target lead, mercury, cadmium, iron, copper, arsenic and gold."},
  {t:"Calcium binding makes the chelator multidentate", correct:false, why:"Multidentate describes one chelator binding one metal atom at several points."}],
 teach:"A chelator must bind the toxic metal more strongly than the body's own macromolecules do. If it also bound positive ions the body needs, such as calcium, or the magnesium in some enzymes, it would strip them away. So the ideal chelator has low binding affinity for endogenous ions like calcium.",
 quote:"the ideal chelator, like I just said, has low binding affinities for other endogenous ions like calcium. You don't want it to bind up the things you need.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Heavy Metal Chelators’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-116", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"antibody", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-antivenom-delay-rural", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient bitten by a snake in rural South Texas waits hours for antivenom. Which explains the wait?",
 options:[
  {t:"It is too expensive for small hospitals to stock", correct:true, why:"Her reason: antivenoms are not stocked in every small rural hospital because they are too expensive, so the patient waits for a large hospital, such as those in San Antonio, to send it."},
  {t:"Each dose must be raised in an animal after the bite", correct:false, why:"Animals are inoculated to make the antibodies beforehand; the wait is about where the stock is."},
  {t:"It must be monoclonal, and few are made", correct:false, why:"Many antivenoms are still polyclonal and in use; few monoclonal antivenoms exist yet."},
  {t:"It must be made from the patient's own blood", correct:false, why:"Polyclonal antivenom is collected from an inoculated animal."},
  {t:"It must first be checked for type III hypersensitivity", correct:false, why:"Serum sickness is a reaction that can follow polyclonal antivenom; it is not a test that delays giving it."}],
 teach:"Antivenoms are expensive, so small rural hospitals do not stock them. A patient far from a large hospital has to wait for the antivenom to be brought in. Hospitals know which other hospitals hold antivenom and how much, and there have been shortages, such as coral snake antivenom in recent years.",
 quote:"Um, and these antivenoms are not stopped in every little small rural hospital. It's too expensive. So if somebody gets bit by a snake down in South Texas, and they're not close to a big hospital, you got to wait for, like, the hospitals here in San Antonio to get the venom to wherever the patient is.",
 note:"The transcript has \"not stopped\"; the sense is \"not stocked\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Antivenoms and Antibodies’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-117", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"apply",
 concept:"warfarin-epoxide-reductase", dupOf:"L16-050", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Warfarin does not act on vitamin K-dependent carboxylase, yet it lowers production of active coagulation factors. What explains this?",
 options:[
  {t:"Less active vitamin K is regenerated for it", correct:true, why:"Her chain: warfarin inhibits epoxide reductase, so less vitamin K is reformed, and with less active vitamin K fewer coagulation factors are made."},
  {t:"Warfarin competes with vitamin K at the carboxylase", correct:false, why:"She said it would be easier if warfarin acted on the carboxylase, but it does not."},
  {t:"Warfarin breaks down coagulation factors in the blood", correct:false, why:"Warfarin acts on the vitamin K cycle in the liver, not on circulating factors."},
  {t:"Warfarin turns active vitamin K into its epoxide", correct:false, why:"The carboxylase reaction turns vitamin K into the epoxide; warfarin blocks the step back."},
  {t:"Warfarin inhibits the carboxylase's supply of epoxide", correct:false, why:"Confuses the two forms: the epoxide is the inactive, used-up vitamin K; the carboxylase needs the active form that epoxide reductase regenerates."}],
 teach:"Vitamin K is the cofactor that lets vitamin K-dependent carboxylase carboxylate, and so activate, some coagulation factors. Using vitamin K turns it into an inactive epoxide, and epoxide reductase reduces it back to the active form. Warfarin inhibits epoxide reductase, so less active vitamin K is available, and fewer active coagulation factors are made.",
 quote:"it inhibits the activity of the epoxide reductase. Therefore, you reduce the amount of vitamin K being reformed. Therefore, if you don't have as much active vitamin K, you don't make as much coagulation factors.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway’ (warfarin figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-118", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"apply",
 concept:"why-liver-disease-overanticoagulation", tags:["smith","why"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A patient awaiting a liver transplant is still on warfarin and has bruises all over her arms. Which explains the bruising, and what was given?",
 options:[
  {t:"Her liver makes few factors and warfarin cuts them further; vitamin K", correct:true, why:"Her story: the liver does the work of creating coagulation factors, this patient's liver was bad, and she was still on warfarin, so she was over-anticoagulated; the nurses gave her a vitamin K injection."},
  {t:"Warfarin is not metabolized in liver failure and acts as a toxin; charcoal", correct:false, why:"Charcoal prevents absorption; the problem was too few coagulation factors, treated with vitamin K."},
  {t:"Her liver makes too much vitamin K epoxide reductase; more warfarin", correct:false, why:"More warfarin would add to the over-anticoagulation."},
  {t:"The transplant drugs block the carboxylase; methylene blue", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue regenerates hemoglobin in the cyanide scheme."},
  {t:"Her liver releases free iron into the blood; deferoxamine", correct:false, why:"Deferoxamine chelates iron; it does not treat bleeding."}],
 teach:"Coagulation factors are made by the liver, so patients with bad livers have disturbed coagulation profiles. Warfarin lowers active coagulation factors further by limiting active vitamin K. Bruising signals over-anticoagulation, and giving vitamin K bypasses the blocked step.",
 quote:"I used to do work in a, a liver transplantation clinic, and we'd have patients with really disturbed, uh, uh, coagulation profiles because their livers aren't good, and all this work of creating your coagulation factors is done by your liver.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-119", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ir-six-hour-window", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why can an asymptomatic patient found more than 6 hours after taking an immediate-release calcium channel blocker, or an immediate-release beta blocker other than sotalol, probably be managed at home?",
 options:[
  {t:"All the drug is absorbed by then, so symptoms would show", correct:true, why:"Her reason: all that drug should have been absorbed into their system and they should be displaying symptoms; if it has been 6 hours with no symptoms, they are OK."},
  {t:"The drug has been completely eliminated by 6 hours", correct:false, why:"The point is absorption, not elimination: by 6 hours it has all been absorbed."},
  {t:"Immediate-release tablets clump in the gut and are released late", correct:false, why:"Clumping into a bezoar is the problem with extended-release products."},
  {t:"Charcoal given at home has bound the rest", correct:false, why:"Charcoal is not given at home."},
  {t:"These drugs are not toxic in overdose", correct:false, why:"Within the window the patient could still respond very badly."}],
 teach:"An immediate-release product is absorbed within about 6 hours. Found within 6 hours and asymptomatic, the patient goes to the hospital, since the drug could still take effect. Found after 6 hours with no symptoms, the absorbed drug would already have caused symptoms, so the patient can probably be watched at home with the poison center. On 10/20 she added that a patient still asymptomatic before that time point should be brought into the hospital, especially if no one can confirm how much was taken.",
 quote:"If they're past 6 hours for both the calcium channel blockers and the beta blockers, and it's an immediate release product, this guidance document is saying if they're not symptomatic after 6 hours of post ingestion, they're going to be OK. In other words, All that drug should have been absorbed into their system, they should be displaying symptoms",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19; transcript 10/20 (amount unknown)"},

/* ================= Part (b): poisoning table reasoning (her key) ================= */
{id:"TBL3-101", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"metabolic", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-alc-moa", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"In ethylene glycol poisoning, why does inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase with ethanol or fomepizole protect the kidneys?",
 options:[
  {t:"It stops formation of the metabolites that crystallize", correct:true, why:"Her key: ethylene glycol active metabolites can crystallize in the microvasculature, and renal toxicity is common; the antidotes inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase, the first step that makes them."},
  {t:"It chelates the oxalic acid crystals in the kidney", correct:false, why:"Chelators bind metals; ethanol and fomepizole act on the enzyme."},
  {t:"It dissolves crystals that have already formed", correct:false, why:"Blocking the enzyme prevents new metabolites; dissolving crystals was not described."},
  {t:"It traps ethylene glycol in the urine by raising the urine pH", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is the salicylate treatment."},
  {t:"It antagonizes ethylene glycol at its receptor", correct:false, why:"The toxicity comes from metabolites, and the antidotes act on the enzyme that makes them."}],
 teach:"Alcohol dehydrogenase converts ethylene glycol to glycoaldehyde, and further steps lead to oxalic acid. Oxalic acid can form small crystals in the microvasculature of organs such as the kidneys and liver, blocking blood flow. Ethanol or fomepizole inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, so those active metabolites are not made.",
 quote:"When you have small microvascular, um, vascularization of these organs, sometimes the oxalic acid can form into little crystals and blocks blood flow and damages those organs.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"TBL3-102", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cns", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-benzo-moa", dupOf:"TBL3-013", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why does flumazenil reverse the central nervous system (CNS) depression of a benzodiazepine overdose?",
 options:[
  {t:"It competes with the benzodiazepine at its receptor", correct:true, why:"Her key: competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor. On 10/19: the benzodiazepine agonizes the GABA receptor, and flumazenil antagonizes it."},
  {t:"It adds to GABA signaling as a second agonist", correct:false, why:"She corrected this slip in class: the benzodiazepine is the agonist and flumazenil is the antagonist."},
  {t:"It binds the benzodiazepine in the blood for renal elimination", correct:false, why:"Binding the drug for renal elimination is the digoxin antibody fragments row."},
  {t:"It speeds benzodiazepine metabolism as a cofactor", correct:false, why:"Cofactors enhance metabolism in the cyanide and acetaminophen examples."},
  {t:"It blocks the opioid receptor", correct:false, why:"That is naloxone."}],
 teach:"In overdose the benzodiazepine agonizes the GABA-A (benzodiazepine) receptor, causing CNS depression with relatively normal vitals. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at that receptor, so it blocks the effect of the agonist. Her key's caution: it can cause withdrawal in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient.",
 quote:"So flonazanil is our treatment. It's antagonizing the G GABA receptors, because the benzos are an overdose and they agonize that receptor, OK?",
 note:"On 10/19 she briefly said the roles backwards and then corrected herself; keyed on her correction and her key. The transcript renders flumazenil as \"flonazanil\".",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Benzodiazepines’; Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor antagonist’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"TBL3-103", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cns", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-opioid-moa", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Naloxone reverses an opioid overdose, yet in a chronic opioid user it can trigger withdrawal. Which explains both?",
 options:[
  {t:"It competitively blocks the opioid receptors the opioid acts on", correct:true, why:"Her key: competitive antagonist of opioid receptors; special consideration: it can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users. The same receptor blockade underlies the reversal and the withdrawal."},
  {t:"It binds the opioid in the blood and clears it through the kidney", correct:false, why:"Binding and renal clearance is the digoxin antibody fragments row; naloxone acts at the receptor."},
  {t:"It speeds opioid metabolism to an inactive product", correct:false, why:"Naloxone is a receptor antagonist, not a metabolic cofactor."},
  {t:"It acts as an agonist at a second opioid receptor", correct:false, why:"Naloxone is an antagonist."},
  {t:"It blocks the GABA-A receptor", correct:false, why:"That receptor belongs to flumazenil and the benzodiazepines."}],
 teach:"Opioid overdose causes somnolence, coma, slowed heart and breathing rates and small pupils. Naloxone is a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors, so it blocks the opioid's effect there. In a chronic opioid user that blockade can trigger withdrawal, the same concern her key lists for flumazenil in benzodiazepine dependence.",
 quote:"using naloxone to treat an opioid overdose, right? Naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor",
 note:"Her key gives the mechanism (competitive opioid receptor antagonist) and \"can trigger withdrawal\" in separate cells; linking the withdrawal to that same receptor block is reasoning from those two entries, not her spoken words.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Opioids’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"TBL3-104", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cardio", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-bb-glucagon", dupOf:"TBL3-019", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"A patient with a beta-blocker overdose is bradycardic. Why is glucagon on the antidote list?",
 options:[
  {t:"It raises cardiac cAMP to raise heart rate", correct:true, why:"Her key: glucagon increases cardiac cellular cAMP to increase heart rate, which counters the bradycardia."},
  {t:"It inhibits insulin release from the pancreas", correct:false, why:"That is octreotide's listed mechanism in the sulfonylurea row; glucagon's listed mechanism in the beta-blocker row is to increase cardiac cellular cAMP to increase heart rate."},
  {t:"It drives calcium into the cardiac cells", correct:false, why:"That is calcium."},
  {t:"It acts as a sink for a lipophilic drug", correct:false, why:"That is IV 20% fatty acid emulsion."},
  {t:"It blocks muscarinic receptors on the heart", correct:false, why:"Muscarinic antagonism is atropine's listed mechanism in the organophosphate row."}],
 teach:"Beta-blocker overdose causes hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression. Glucagon is listed because it increases cardiac cellular cAMP, which increases heart rate. The calcium channel blocker row lists the same antidotes.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},

{id:"TBL3-105", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cardio", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-bb-match", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Beta-blocker overdose presents with hypotension and bradycardia. Which pairing matches atropine and the vasopressors to the sign each is listed to counter?",
 options:[
  {t:"Atropine: bradycardia; vasopressors: hypotension", correct:true, why:"Her key: atropine increases heart rate; vasopressors increase blood pressure."},
  {t:"Atropine: hypotension; vasopressors: bradycardia", correct:false, why:"Swaps the two: atropine's listed mechanism is to increase heart rate, which answers bradycardia; vasopressors increase blood pressure."},
  {t:"Atropine: altered mental status; vasopressors: bradycardia", correct:false, why:"Atropine's listed mechanism is to increase heart rate."},
  {t:"Atropine: respiratory depression; vasopressors: hypotension", correct:false, why:"Atropine's listed mechanism is to increase heart rate."},
  {t:"Both: altered mental status", correct:false, why:"Neither mechanism in the key addresses mental status."}],
 teach:"Each antidote on the beta-blocker list is aimed at a sign of the overdose. Atropine increases heart rate, against the bradycardia; vasopressors increase blood pressure, against the hypotension. In the organophosphate row atropine appears again, there as a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},

{id:"TBL3-106", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cardio", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-bb-fe", dupOf:"TBL3-022", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Intravenous (IV) 20% fatty acid emulsion is on the beta-blocker antidote list. Why might it help with one beta blocker and not another?",
 options:[
  {t:"It is a sink only for a drug lipophilic enough", correct:true, why:"Her key: acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough."},
  {t:"It works only for sustained-release products", correct:false, why:"Formulation decides the guideline time windows, not the emulsion's effect."},
  {t:"It works only for drugs with a small volume of distribution", correct:false, why:"Small volume of distribution is the condition for extracorporeal removal."},
  {t:"It works only when given within 6 hours", correct:false, why:"The 6-hour mark is the immediate-release window in the guidelines."},
  {t:"It works only for drugs that bind antibodies", correct:false, why:"Mixes in digoxin Fab; the emulsion holds a drug by its lipophilicity, not by antibody binding."}],
 teach:"The emulsion acts as a sink: the drug moves into it. That only happens if the drug is lipophilic enough, so the condition in the key is lipophilicity. The calcium channel blocker row lists the same antidote with the same mechanism.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},

{id:"TBL3-107", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cardio", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-dig-moa", dupOf:"TBL3-030", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which explains how digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab) end a digoxin overdose?",
 options:[
  {t:"They bind digoxin; the complex is renally eliminated", correct:true, why:"Her key: binds digoxin, will be renally eliminated. The inactivation slide: the inactivator binds the toxin, prevents its interaction with target tissues, and the complex is cleared."},
  {t:"They block digoxin's receptor on the heart", correct:false, why:"The fragments bind the drug itself, not its target."},
  {t:"They chelate the excess potassium that digoxin overdose raises", correct:false, why:"Chelators bind metals; elevated potassium is a symptom, not the target of Fab."},
  {t:"They speed the liver's metabolism of digoxin", correct:false, why:"Fab is an inactivator, not a metabolic cofactor."},
  {t:"They make the urine basic to trap digoxin", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping is the sodium bicarbonate treatment for weak acids."}],
 teach:"Digoxin immune Fab is an antibody made to grab digoxin. It binds the drug so the drug cannot interact with its target, and the antibody-digoxin complex is renally eliminated. Since the bound digoxin is still measured, total serum digoxin rises after Fab and cannot guide further doses.",
 quote:"One form of antibody made to grab digoxin that somebody's overdosed on, it grabs it and nicely clears it out of the blood.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Digoxin’; Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"TBL3-108", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cns", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-tca-moa", dupOf:"TBL3-034", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"A tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose patient has an elongated QRS interval. Why is sodium bicarbonate given?",
 options:[
  {t:"It counteracts inhibition of cardiac sodium channels", correct:true, why:"Her key: sodium bicarbonate counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels; it is listed for the elongated QRS interval."},
  {t:"It traps the TCA in basic urine for renal elimination", correct:false, why:"Renal ion trapping is the bicarbonate mechanism in the salicylates row."},
  {t:"It raises blood pressure as a vasopressor", correct:false, why:"Dopamine or norepinephrine are listed for blood pressure."},
  {t:"It replenishes glutathione", correct:false, why:"That is N-acetylcysteine for acetaminophen."},
  {t:"It binds the TCA in the plasma", correct:false, why:"Binding the drug is the digoxin antibody fragments row."}],
 teach:"Among the TCA overdose signs are an elongated QRS interval and reduced cardiac contractility. Her key gives sodium bicarbonate for the elongated QRS, and its mechanism there is to counteract the inhibition of cardiac sodium channels. The same drug in the salicylate row works differently, by renal ion trapping.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’"},

{id:"TBL3-109", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"cns", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-tca-moa", dupOf:"TBL3-034", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Why are IV fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine listed for tricyclic antidepressant overdose?",
 options:[
  {t:"Supportive care, to maintain blood pressure", correct:true, why:"Her key: these options are mostly supportive care, trying to maintain blood pressure."},
  {t:"They antagonize the TCA at its receptor", correct:false, why:"No receptor antagonist is listed for TCAs."},
  {t:"They speed renal elimination of the TCA", correct:false, why:"Treats fluids as a kinetic treatment; the key calls them supportive care, holding up blood pressure while the drug is still present."},
  {t:"They counteract sodium channel inhibition", correct:false, why:"That is sodium bicarbonate's listed mechanism."},
  {t:"They reverse the dilated pupils and dry mouth", correct:false, why:"Those are listed signs, but fluids and pressors are aimed at the vasodilation and weak contractility that lower blood pressure."}],
 teach:"TCA overdose can bring vasodilation and reduced cardiac contractility, among other signs. IV fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine are supportive care that tries to maintain blood pressure; they do not act on the TCA itself. Sodium bicarbonate is the one listed treatment aimed at the drug's effect on cardiac sodium channels.",
 note:"Her key lists vasodilation and reduced cardiac contractility among the signs and says the fluids and pressors are supportive care trying to maintain blood pressure; linking those signs to the low blood pressure is an inference from the two cells.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’"},

{id:"TBL3-110", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"metabolic", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-sal-moa", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"What would happen to the renal elimination of salicylate if the urine were left at its normal pH?",
 options:[
  {t:"Salicylate would stay uncharged and be reabsorbed", correct:true, why:"Her words: at normal urine pH these mildly acidic poisons are not charged, so they are reabsorbed; IV sodium bicarbonate makes the urine basic and charges them."},
  {t:"Salicylate would be negatively charged and trapped anyway", correct:false, why:"The negative charge comes from raising the urine pH with bicarbonate."},
  {t:"Salicylate would crystallize in the kidney", correct:false, why:"Crystals in small vessels belong to ethylene glycol's metabolites."},
  {t:"Salicylate would be cleared faster than with bicarbonate", correct:false, why:"Bicarbonate is given to enhance elimination."},
  {t:"Salicylate would be filtered at the glomerulus less", correct:false, why:"It is filtered either way; the issue is reabsorption from the forming urine."}],
 teach:"Salicylates are mildly acidic; they are filtered into the urine but, uncharged at normal urine pH, they are reabsorbed and the poisoning continues. Her key lists IV sodium bicarbonate with the mechanism renal ion trapping to enhance elimination. Bicarbonate raises urine pH, the salicylate takes a negative charge, and the reabsorbing transporters do not take it back.",
 quote:"They're in the urine, and at normal urine pH, they are not charged, OK? But if we will try to make the urine more basic by giving the patient sodium bicarb by IV, the sodium bicarb goes into the urine and will charge that acid, and it can't be reabsorbed when it's charged.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Salicylates’; Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Renal Elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"TBL3-111", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"metabolic", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-su-moa", dupOf:"TBL3-047", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Sulfonylurea or meglitinide overdose causes hypoglycemia. Why does octreotide treat it?",
 options:[
  {t:"It inhibits insulin release from the pancreas", correct:true, why:"Her key: octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, against the hypoglycemia listed for this overdose."},
  {t:"It stimulates insulin release from the pancreas", correct:false, why:"Reversed; more insulin would lower glucose further."},
  {t:"It increases cardiac cellular cAMP", correct:false, why:"That is glucagon in the beta-blocker row."},
  {t:"It binds the sulfonylurea for renal elimination", correct:false, why:"Binding for renal elimination is the digoxin antibody fragments row."},
  {t:"It competitively blocks the sulfonylurea's receptor", correct:false, why:"The key lists inhibition of insulin release, not receptor antagonism."}],
 teach:"The only symptom her key lists for sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose is hypoglycemia, so the antidote targets insulin, not the drug. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas; with less insulin released, the hypoglycemia is countered. It is not a receptor antagonist or a binder of the drug, which separates it from naloxone, flumazenil and digoxin Fab.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’"},

{id:"TBL3-112", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"ironop", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-iron-moa", tags:["smith","why"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Deferoxamine treats iron overdose. Which approach is this, and why does it work?",
 options:[
  {t:"Inactivation: it chelates circulating ferric iron", correct:true, why:"Her key: chelates the circulating ferric iron. The inactivation slides: a chelator binds the metal so it cannot act, and the complex is readily cleared."},
  {t:"Pharmacological: it antagonizes iron at its receptor", correct:false, why:"Confuses a chelator with a receptor antagonist such as naloxone; deferoxamine binds the iron itself."},
  {t:"Kinetic: it prevents iron absorption from the gut", correct:false, why:"Deferoxamine acts on circulating iron, after absorption."},
  {t:"Pharmacological: it removes iron from an enzyme's active site", correct:false, why:"Removal from an active site is the pralidoxime and methemoglobin examples."},
  {t:"Kinetic: it traps iron in the urine by raising pH", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping is for weakly acidic poisons such as salicylates."}],
 teach:"Inactivation is used once a toxin has entered the circulation. A heavy metal chelator binds the metal with high affinity, the complex has no toxicity (she changed the slide's \"low\" to \"no\"), and it is readily cleared. Deferoxamine chelates circulating ferric iron; deferasirox is the other iron chelator, and it can be given orally.",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Iron’; Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slides ‘Inactivation of Poisons’, ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Heavy Metal Chelators’"},

{id:"TBL3-113", lecture:"TBL3", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"TBL3", sub:"ironop", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tbl-op-antidotes", tags:["smith","why"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why are two antidotes, atropine and pralidoxime, listed for organophosphate (OP) insecticide poisoning?",
 options:[
  {t:"Atropine blocks excess acetylcholine; pralidoxime frees the enzyme", correct:true, why:"Her key: atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist; pralidoxime removes the OP from acetylcholinesterase. On 10/19 she called pralidoxime a second way to treat OP exposures."},
  {t:"Atropine frees the enzyme; pralidoxime blocks the receptor", correct:false, why:"Swaps the two: atropine works at the receptor on the acetylcholine already in excess; pralidoxime works on the inhibited enzyme."},
  {t:"Both block muscarinic receptors, at different doses", correct:false, why:"Misses pralidoxime's site: it pulls the OP off acetylcholinesterase, so the enzyme can break acetylcholine down again."},
  {t:"Pralidoxime chelates the OP; atropine speeds the OP's metabolism", correct:false, why:"Borrows mechanisms from other rows: chelation is for metals such as iron, and atropine is a receptor antagonist, not a metabolic cofactor."},
  {t:"Pralidoxime works on every OP; atropine works only before the OP ages", correct:false, why:"Aging limits pralidoxime: once the OP has aged on the enzyme, pralidoxime cannot pull it off."}],
 teach:"An OP inhibits acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up and causes the excess listed as SLUDGE or DUMBELLS. Atropine antagonizes the acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors; pralidoxime pulls the OP off acetylcholinesterase so the enzyme works again. Pralidoxime only works for OPs that have not aged.",
 quote:"So, pralodoxine is a second way we can treat organophosphate exposures. Sometimes we can pull the organophosphate off the acetylcholinesterase and get it cleared out of the body.",
 note:"The transcript renders pralidoxime as \"pralodoxine\".",
 cite:"PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’; transcript 10/19"}
);
